{
 "id": "HB-07",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-hb-07-719f8f6e3a",
 "endReason": "escalated",
 "customerTurns": 5,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "hob not working properly"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the pan not suiting induction. Many hobs have a key / child lock (often a key or padlock symbol) that stops the zones coming on — the manual shows which button to hold. Was the lock on (and is it off now), or was there no lock on?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It's not induction, it's a Zanussi ceramic hob. Only the rear right zone isn't working, the other three are fine. No lock on as far as I know."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is that zone's element or its control. Try the zone again. Is it heating now, or still not?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Still not heating, no red glow at all on that ring. The display shows the power level fine though."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Check the hob's isolator switch (often a large switch on the wall or in a cupboard) is on, and look at the fuse box for a breaker that has switched off. If a breaker keeps tripping, don't keep resetting it. Was the isolator or a breaker off (and is it sorted), or were they all on?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "No breaker has tripped and the other three zones work fine, so the power must be on. It's just that rear right ring."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Could you tell me a bit more about what it is doing, and when it happens?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It's the rear right zone on my Zanussi ceramic hob. It stopped heating last week, and it had been slow to heat before that. There was also a slight burnt smell from that ring once a couple of weeks ago, but not since."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nA burning or hot-plastic smell, smoke, scorching or melting can mean an electrical fault or a fire risk. Stop using it, switch it off and unplug it (or turn it off at the fuse box), and don't run it again — not even as a test — until it has been checked by a qualified engineer. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
